                <p class="fw-bold" style="font-size: 50px;">About Glaciers</p>
                <div class="d-flex flex-column justify-content-center">
                    <p class="fs-4" style="text-align: justify;">
Glaciers are bodies of ice that have separated themselves from ice sheets and are flowing in a downward direction under the influence of gravity. They have formed over hundreds of years through repeated compression of layers of snow. 
Usually found in polar and high-altitude regions and contain approx. 69% to 75% of the Earth’s fresh water supply. 
One important classification to be noted between different cryosphere landscapes and icebergs is the size. Ice caps hold at most 50,000 sq. km, while an ice mass as big as 100 sq m can be classified as an iceberg. So while every ice cap can be classified as an iceberg, the opposite need not be true.
                    </p>

<h2>How it’s made</h2>
<p class="fs-4" style="text-align: justify;">
Glaciers begin to form when snow remains in the same area year-round, where enough snow accumulates to transform into ice. Each year, new layers of snow bury and compress the previous layers. This compression forces the snow to recrystallise, initially forming grains similar to the size and shape of sugar grains. 
Gradually, the grains grow larger, and the air pockets between the grains get smaller, causing the snow to slowly compact and increase in density. After about a year, the snow turns into firn—an intermediate state between snow and glacier ice. At this point, it is about two-thirds as dense as water. Over time, larger ice crystals become so compressed that any air pockets between them are very tiny. In very old glacier ice, crystals can reach the size of an adult fist. For most glaciers, this process takes more than a hundred years.
</p>

<h2>Significance of Ice Caps and Glaciers.</h2>
<p class="fs-4" style="text-align: justify;">
Ice sheets and glaciers hold great ecological significance in the biosphere, as the glaciers often control certain currents, hold a freshwater supply, and are essential for planetary stability and, in the greater picture, the existence of life on Earth.
They are not only a water and power supply but also of great importance to the landscape. When a glacier moves, it creates abrasion on the land, creating valleys, fjords, cirques, etc. They also grind rocks into fine powders, which, when mixed into the water, enter the food chain as nutrients.
</p>

<h2>Need to study glaciers</h2>
<p class="fs-4" style="text-align: justify;">
Studying glaciers is urgent because they serve as early warning systems for global climate change and directly control future water supplies and sea levels. Because ice reacts immediately to shifts in temperature and snowfall, tracking glaciers gives scientists a clear, physical record of how fast the planet is warming. Beyond tracking temperature, this research protects water security for billions of people who rely on seasonal meltwater for drinking, farming, and hydroelectric power. Monitoring shrinking ice also helps coastal cities prepare for rising sea levels and allows experts to predict dangerous natural disasters, such as Glacial Lake Outburst Floods and landslides, before they strike downstream communities.

To understand these changes, researchers across several major scientific fields study glaciers from different angles. Glaciology leads this effort by examining the physics, movement, and annual mass balance of snow and ice, while palaeoclimatology looks backwards by drilling deep ice cores to analyse trapped ancient air bubbles and volcanic ash. Meanwhile, hydrology tracks how meltwater feeds major river systems and reservoirs, and oceanography studies how cold freshwater runoff alters ocean currents, marine nutrients, and global weather patterns. Geomorphology and geology also play key roles by showing how moving ice carves valleys and shapes Earth's surface over time. Finally, astrobiology examines the extreme microbes living inside Earth's glaciers to help scientists search for life on icy moons elsewhere in the solar system.
</p>

<h2>A world without glaciers</h2>
<p class="fs-4" style="text-align: justify;">
If all glaciers and ice sheets on Earth melted away entirely, global sea levels would rise by roughly 65 to 70 meters (about 215 to 230 feet), permanently redrawing the world's coastlines and submerging major cities. Even before total disappearance, ongoing glacial melt is already creating severe global problems. Rising oceans are increasing coastal flooding and pushing saltwater into freshwater aquifers and coastal farmlands. In mountain regions, rapid melting temporarily swells rivers before pushing them past a critical tipping point known as "peak water," after which summer water supplies drop sharply. At the same time, retreating ice destabilises steep slopes and forms fragile glacial lakes, triggering deadly floods and landslides. Furthermore, as bright white ice vanishes, darker land and ocean surfaces absorb more solar heat, accelerating global warming in a dangerous feedback loop.
In a world completely without glaciers, the consequences for human civilisation and ecosystems would be catastrophic. A 70-meter rise in sea level would swallow major coastal hubs like Mumbai, New York, London, Tokyo, and Shanghai, while submerging entire low-lying island nations and vital river deltas, displacing hundreds of millions of people. Simultaneously, iconic rivers such as the Ganges, Indus, Yangtze, and Rhine would lose their steady summer meltwater, shrinking to seasonal trickles and crippling drinking water, agriculture, and hydroelectric power for billions. The massive influx of cold freshwater into the oceans would also weaken major currents like the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation, triggering erratic global weather patterns, disrupted monsoons, and extreme heatwaves. Finally, ecosystems from high mountain peaks to the polar regions would collapse as cold-water fish, polar wildlife, and nutrient-dependent marine food webs lose the icy environments they need to survive.
</p>
